  "companion_answers": {
    "LG01": "CHCl3: the 3 counts Cl atoms; 4 + 1 + 3(7) = 26 electrons. NO2-: the 2 counts O atoms, the overall -1 adds one electron; 5 + 2(6) + 1 = 18.",
    "LG02": "See NCl3 record and staged worked example. Three N-Cl bonds, one N lone pair, three lone pairs on each Cl; 26 electrons and 3 shared pairs.",
    "LG03": "NH3: 8 valence electrons, three N-H single bonds, 3 shared pairs, one N lone pair, no H lone pairs. H has a duet. Cl contributes seven electrons rather than one and keeps three lone pairs; N retains its same three-bond plus lone-pair arrangement.",
    "LG04": "The correct monomer budget is 24, all spent on 3 bonds (6 electrons) and 9 Cl lone pairs (18 electrons). An added Al lone pair gives 26 electrons and an invalid charge ledger; it is not neutral AlCl3. The monomer allows six electrons around Al. Moving an existing pair changes allocation without changing the total.",
    "LG05": "CO2 has 4 shared pairs in two double bonds, 2 bonding domains around C, and zero C lone pairs. Shared pairs count electron pairs; domains count regions pointing to neighbors.",
    "LG06": "See NO2- record and staged worked example. Budget 18; two equivalent contributors; N has one lone pair, two bonded neighbors, three domains, trigonal planar electron geometry and bent molecular shape.",
    "LG07": "CO2: 16 electrons; no C lone pairs, 2 domains, linear electron geometry and molecular shape. NO2-: 18 electrons; one N lone pair, 3 domains, trigonal planar electron geometry, bent molecular shape. Equal neighbor counts do not imply equal domain counts.",
    "LG08": "NH4+ is a polyatomic cation. Budget 5 + 4(1) - 1 = 8; four N-H single bonds; 4 shared pairs; no N lone pair; four domains; tetrahedral electron geometry and shape. N formal charge +1; all H 0; draw brackets and +. Positive charge subtracts one electron; negative charge adds one.",
    "LG09": "NCl3 has 26 valence electrons but 3 shared pairs; CO2 has 2 central domains; NO2- has trigonal planar electron geometry and bent shape; 109.5 degrees is the ideal reference for CF2Cl2, not a promise of identical actual angles; PBr3 gives P an octet whereas PCl5 is commonly drawn with an expanded valence shell."
  },
